I'm joined today by Sara bunin the nor  an associate professor of contemporary  Jewish Studies at Hebrew Union College  in Los Angeles and author of becoming  from how newcomers learn the language  and culture of Orthodox Judaism uh Sarah  so great to talk to you you know we've  we've interviewed a number of different  people generally in the world of  linguistics and we've talked about the  different dialects and accents that  exist in the United States but one that  I've increasingly been reading about is  what sort of colloquially known as the  Jewish accent and I think that there's  many people who sort of intuitively know  what it is that we're talking about but  how is this defined academically what  are we talking about when we say the  Jewish accent well I would say there are  two different types of Jewish accents  the one that you're probably referring  to is the New York Jewish accent that's  immigrant influenced by immigrant  Yiddish and probably spoken by the  children and grandchildren of immigrants  and then there's the accent of Orthodox  Jews throughout the english-speaking  world where their English is influenced  by Yiddish grammatical structures and  Yiddish sounds in a slightly different  way than the immigrant generation and  their children and grandchildren so I  have friends who who sometimes will  disagree about when you're talking about  the the often sort of uh immigrant at  New York associated so-called Jewish  accent there's disagreement as to  whether we're talking about something  distinct and unique in in terms of a  Jewish accent or whether we're really  talking about a particular sort of  neighborhood be it Lower East Side or  Brooklyn version of just a New York  accent is one or the other view right or  wrong yeah I think you're absolutely  right that the the New York accent is  sometimes seen as a Jewish accent and  that has to do with the large New York  Jewish population  it but um I I would say that there are  certainly Jews who are not from New York  who sound like they are from New Yorker  who have elements of a New York accent  and when we talk about that is this a  phenomena that was first identified sort  of in the early 20th century with the  wave of immigration to certain parts of  New York City or what other sort of  historical connections have been  associated with this yes the historical  wave of immigration to New York from  Eastern Europe between 1880 and 1924  played a big role in the establishment  of a distinctive Jewish sound you might  call it an ethnic way of speaking and  you can see this in other ethnic  communities throughout the United States  in in different regions such as the  German population in Wisconsin or the  african-american population in various  cities or Latino population in various  cities that if if an ethnic group has a  large concentration in a given area then  they will likely have distinctive  features in their language now you've  done some research which has revealed  that even among Jews who are not from  New York themselves and even whose  parents did not live or from New York  that there's a much higher likelihood  that they will exhibit some of the  characteristics that are often common to  this sort of Jewish accent that we're  talking about and this might include the  pronunciation of Florida versus Florida  and some other even phrases online  versus in line etc how do we explain  this how do we understand that right and  I personally do that myself my parents  are lived part of their childhood in New  York and the New York area and I didn't  grow up in New York but I say Florida  and horrible and I think it has to do  with the New York centered social  networks  that many  have that if if many Jews interact with  other people who have spent time in New  York or who grew up in New York then  these kinds of New York features will  spread and that's true in the non  Orthodox circles that I'm a part of but  it's also even more so in the Orthodox  world where there's a particular feature  I call it the non raising of pre nasal  ah so that is saying where it's like  candle and camp instead of candle and  camp right and and that feature is  common it I I think it started in New  York as a response to the New York valve  and then among Orthodox Jews and then  spread all throughout the country so  that people in Orthodox communities  around the u.s. have many of them have  that feature I've sort of anecdotally  observed during my time living in New  York City up until about a year ago and  also now living in Boston both cities  that have particular accents that are  that are often portrayed in media  etcetera that increasingly there seems  to be a sort of class element to  particularly with younger people who are  hearing more English in media and and  sort of non localized English accents uh  that it seems as though many of these  accents are becoming less common  particularly outside of lower middle  class individuals are my anecdotal  observations accurate in your mind or or  or maybe not yes there is certainly  evidence that the New York accent is  decreasing to some extent and you're  absolutely right that social class plays  an important role in how local people  tend to sound and when you when you sort  of try to think going forward I think  that there's you know we've we spoke for  example to the linguist William Leigh  Bob about uh his analysis of the  different accents that exist in the u.s.  what can we say more generally about I  mean are there it it it seems to me we  can't necessarily say that all of these  accents are going to be decreasing over  time  I'm maybe depending on how isolated  certain communities are I mean we know  that the Appalachian way of speaking  maybe is going to persist more strongly  because it's it's maybe a more isolated  community than maybe your average New  Yorker I don't know I mean is there some  trend that we can observe right well the  New York accent has been shown to be  decreasing to some extent but other  regional accents around the country are  very strong for example in the Midwest  saying salad instead of salad is is  actually increasing and is widening in  the areas that it's used in so and the  California vowels are also becoming more  distinct in certain ways so so right I  don't think it's a nationwide trend of  the decrease in regional accents but  certainly the New York accent in  particular has diminished to some extent  do you have any opinion as to whether  media portrayals of accents uh tend to  sort of reinforce them in some way or  does it make others may be more aware of  speaking differences such that they  might try to avoid a localizing accent  maybe it's more of a psychology question  than anything else but you have some  sense of that yeah I think we don't have  enough evidence to answer the question  of the role of the media in influencing  the spread or decrease in regional  accents but we do have some idea of how  certain accents can become seen as a  particular accent because of the media  or for example in Pittsburgh saying  going downtown for downtown is has  become a has gained cachet and that  probably is influenced by the media and  by t-shirts that say those kinds of  phrases and that highlight the  distinctive Pittsburgh way of speaking  well it is absolutely fascinating I'll  remind you the book is becoming from how  newcomers learn the language and culture  of Orthodox Judaism we've been speaking  with the  books author Sarab union bin or who's an  associate professor of contemporary  Jewish Studies at Hebrew Union College  in Los Angeles Sarah thanks so much for  talking to us thanks so much for having  me
 

